replace('At this common minimum, retained/dense policy headrooms are 2.32/6.88 points: the 0.29-point capacity gap plus the 4.56-point headroom difference equals the 4.85-point policy gain. Preserved rankings give $G=0$, removing the $2rG$ variance term when correctness rates match; Supplement P derives the class-stratified identity. On CIFAR L/14, the selected-policy gain reaches 35.40 points (72.00\\% versus 36.60\\%), including finite admission in 10/10 versus 6/10 partitions.',r'Thus the evidence distinguishes matched-base recognition gains (3.10 points originally; 3.35 after retuning), admission gains without fallback (4.85), and gains that include fewer fallbacks: CIFAR L/14 gives 72.00\% versus 36.60\%, with 10/10 versus 6/10 admissions. Dense accuracy conditional on its six admissions is 61.00\%; comparing this with the retained ten-partition mean is not a paired conditional contrast. At the common minimum, the 0.29-point capacity gap plus the 4.56-point headroom difference equals the 4.85-point policy gain.')
replace(r'For the original retained readout, $G=0$ makes protection coincide with retention. Calibration uses held-out base images, with no extra novel labels beyond five-shot support.',r'For the original retained readout, $G=0$ makes protection coincide with retention. Supplement V holds class-wise correctness rates fixed: removing recovery raises CIFAR L/14 simulated test acceptance from 2.8\% to 90.6\% at cap 40. On actual dense calibration pairs, fixed-marginal recovery reduction changes four of ten rejected candidate tests to accepted with each encoder ($\Delta\log E=.70/1.64$). This coupling diagnostic isolates certification from recognition; it is not a new predictor.')
replace('Source-only calibration supports recognition on this natural shift; target labels further increase admission. Population confidence concerns the calibration distribution. Supplement O tests calibration--query correspondence under CIFAR noise and blur.',r'Fixed-reference moments adapt each query\textquotesingle s score origins and scales while preserving the source decision frame. The zero-target-label results demonstrate empirical transfer of retention and recognition; population confidence remains tied to the calibration distribution. Supplement O tests noise and blur.')
